# Complete coverage, evidence captured online (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-08-08), verbatim:

> **Global Objective: Provide ALL resources, workflows, and attestation to
> COMPLETELY cover the needs of CMMC and other regulated organizations and
> their workforces. We gather the evidence through our online systems.**

This is the product's purpose statement, not a feature request. A regulated
organization does not need a tool that covers the parts that were convenient to
build. It needs every obligation it carries to land somewhere in the product
with a document to read, a workflow that drives it to done, and a record that
survives an assessor asking "show me." Anything the product declines to serve
becomes a spreadsheet, a shared drive, or a binder in a filing cabinet — exactly
the artifacts that fail an assessment interview.

## The rule

> **Every obligation a customer or a member of their workforce carries under a
> framework this product serves must have all three of: the resource the
> obligation needs, the workflow that assigns and drives it to completion, and
> the record that proves it happened. All three live in the product, and the
> record is captured by the product's own online systems. "The customer owns
> that control" is never a reason to ship nothing — the assessor's verdict is
> theirs, the artifact and the record are ours to provide.**

Coverage and honesty are **both** required, simultaneously. This rule says what
the product must **provide**.
[`compliance-claims-honesty.mdc`](compliance-claims-honesty.mdc) says what the
product may **claim**. Complete coverage never upgrades a status to Met, and
honest status reporting never excuses shipping nothing.

## 1. The three-part coverage test

Apply to every requirement in scope. A row missing any column is incomplete
work, whether or not the requirement is "customer-owned."

| Part | Question | Shape it takes here |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Resource** | What does the person need in front of them? | Training document, policy template, procedure, checklist, curriculum, marking guide, notice text |
| **Workflow** | What drives it from assigned to done? | Program schedule, assignment, due date, reminder ladder, approval, review cycle, remediation task, gate |
| **Evidence** | What does the assessor read a year later? | Attestation ledger row, audit event, binder section, attachment with hash, completion report, immutable timestamped record |

Three-part coverage is the working definition of "done" for a compliance
feature. A document library with no assignment is a resource with no workflow.
An assignment with no signed record is a workflow with no evidence.

## 2. Evidence is gathered online, by us

> **The product captures its own evidence.** If proving a control requires the
> customer to keep a spreadsheet, maintain a shared-drive folder, email an
> attachment to an administrator, or file a signed sheet of paper, the product
> has not covered that need.

Every evidence record must be:

| Property | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| **Attributable** | Names the person or service principal, resolved from a real identity, not a free-text field |
| **Timestamped** | UTC, produced by the server, not supplied by the client |
| **Durable** | Persisted in the database, surviving restart, upgrade, and cache clear |
| **Queryable** | Reachable by an API and a report, not only by reading a log file |
| **Append-only where audit requires it** | Attestations and audit events are never updated in place (NIST AU-9) |
| **Exportable** | Leaves the product in a form an assessor accepts, with the honesty framing attached |
| **Integrity-checkable** | Uploaded artifacts carry a content hash so a swapped file is detectable |

Paper and out-of-band paths may exist as a **fallback the product records** (an
operator attests that an offline act occurred, with a reason and an attachment).
They may never be the **only** path. The difference is whether the product ends
up holding the record.

## 3. The workforce is a first-class surface

The directive says "and their workforces." Coverage that only serves the
administrator is half coverage. The person who handles the data needs their own
path: receive the assignment, read the material, and attest — without an
administrator doing it for them, and without needing to understand the framework
to complete the task.

That surface inherits every accessibility and plain-language obligation:
sixth-grade reading level and image-rich original content
([`training-materials-plain-language-and-images.mdc`](training-materials-plain-language-and-images.mdc)),
Section 508 / WCAG 2.0 AA
([`enterprise-compliance.mdc`](enterprise-compliance.mdc) §2), readable contrast
([`readable-read-only-controls.mdc`](readable-read-only-controls.mdc)), and
low-friction authentication that does not weaken the security floor
([`baseline-security-perfect-always.mdc`](baseline-security-perfect-always.mdc)).
A workflow a worker cannot complete without help from IT will not be completed,
and an assessment interview finds that out.

## 4. "Customer-owned" is not an exit

Decompose every "customer-owned" requirement before accepting it as out of
scope:

| Genuinely not ours | Ours to provide anyway |
| --- | --- |
| The assessor's judgment that a control is satisfied | The artifact and record the assessor evaluates |
| The customer's decision about who must do what, how often | The mechanism to express that decision and enforce it |
| A physical act in the world (a lock, a fence, a shredder, a background check) | The policy, the procedure, the checklist, the completion record, the attestation, the evidence attachment |
| A third-party attestation we do not issue | The intake, storage, hashing, expiry tracking, and renewal reminder for it |

Worked reframes. **PE:** we do not install a door; we do provide the visitor-log
workflow, escort attestation, facility-access review cycle, policy template, and
the attachment slot for a badge-system export. **PS:** we do not run a
background check; we do provide the screening checklist, completion record,
onboarding/offboarding workflow, reminder, and the attachment for the provider's
result letter. **AT:** we do not decide the curriculum is adequate for their
environment; we do provide the documents, schedule, assignment, reminder ladder,
signed attestation, and completion report. **MP:** we do not stand over a
shredder; we do provide the destruction procedure, the witnessed-destruction
attestation, and the log.

In each case the product ends up holding evidence the customer would otherwise
keep in a spreadsheet. That is the whole point of the directive.

## 5. Coverage gaps are registered, never silently omitted

When a need cannot be covered in the current release, it goes in the owning gap
document with three things: what the need is, why it is not covered yet, and
what would close it. The canonical registers are
`pim-offline-server/docs/dev/cmmc-800-53-full-gap-analysis.md` and
`cmmc-800-53-closeable-dev-plan.md`.

A need **permanently** outside software's reach is recorded with that reason,
and still gets an **evidence intake path** per §4. "Customer forever" rows are
re-read against §4 before being accepted — several of them are actually "verdict
is theirs, record is ours."

## 6. Forbidden constructs

- Shipping a document library with no assignment, schedule, or completion record
  and describing the requirement as addressed.
- Shipping a workflow whose completion leaves no durable, attributable record.
- Telling a customer, in documentation or UI copy, to track something in a
  spreadsheet, a shared drive, an email thread, or on paper as the only path.
- Accepting a requirement as customer-owned without running the §4
  decomposition, when a resource, workflow, or evidence slot is clearly
  buildable.
- An administrator-only surface for an obligation a workforce member personally
  carries.
- Evidence records that are client-timestamped, mutable in place where audit
  requires append-only, unattributable, or reachable only by reading a log.
- Uploaded artifacts stored without a content hash.
- Reading "COMPLETELY cover" as license to stamp Met, certified, compliant, or
  assessment-ready. Coverage is what we provide; status is what we proved. Both
  rules bind at once.
- Dropping acceptance criteria or verification to claim broader coverage sooner.
  Breadth never buys its way past
  [`essential-ui-actions-must-be-visible.mdc`](essential-ui-actions-must-be-visible.mdc)
  or the sibling proof rules.

## 7. Required agent behavior

1. When planning or implementing a compliance feature, state the three-part
   coverage row explicitly: what the resource is, what the workflow is, and
   where the evidence lands. If a column is empty, say so and say why.
2. When a requirement looks customer-owned, run the §4 decomposition in the open
   before accepting it. Name what remains genuinely theirs.
3. When adding an evidence record, satisfy every property in §2 in the same
   change set. A record added now and made attributable later is a record an
   assessor rejects.
4. Report coverage and status as two separate statements. "Resource, workflow,
   and evidence are live; the assessment verdict remains the assessor's" is
   honest and complete. "Covered, therefore Met" is neither.
5. Update the owning gap register in the same change set when coverage moves.
6. Mirror this rule to `core-assets/.cursor/rules/` when editing the workspace
   copy — the workspace `.cursor` directory is not a git root
   ([`auto-commit-push.mdc`](auto-commit-push.mdc)).

## Worked violation to fix

**Violation.** A release ships a library of awareness documents an operator can
render and download. The gap analysis row for Awareness and Training is updated
to say the product now addresses it. There is no way to assign a document to a
named person, no due date, no reminder, and no record that anyone read anything.
The customer is told to track completion in their HR system.

Two rules broken at once: coverage, because the workflow and evidence columns
are empty; and honesty, because a render was described as addressing a control.

**Fix.** Land the assignment schedule, the reminder ladder, and an append-only
attestation ledger that names the person, the document version, and the UTC
server timestamp. Expose a completion report and feed the evidence into the
assessment binder. Then state both halves plainly: the product now provides the
resource, the workflow, and the record; whether the content is adequate for that
environment, and whether the control is satisfied, remain the customer's and the
assessor's calls.

